# Writing component specs

A component is ONE file in `components/<id>.js` that default-exports a spec. Every output
(React, Astro, HTML/CSS, vanilla JS, Webflow paste) is generated from it. Never hand-edit `dist/`.

Start every spec with `/** @type {import("../src/types").ComponentSpec} */` for type checking.
Types live in `src/types.d.ts`.

## Shape
- `id` kebab-case with a number (`pricing-03`), `prefix` is the id without the dash (`pricing3`).
- `category`, `name` as "Category / Variant", one-sentence `description`.
- `props`: content only. Types: `text`, `link`, `image` (`{ src, alt }`, `priority` for above-the-fold),
  `boolean` (`{ default: true|false }`), `buttons` (array of `{ title, href, variant }`), `list`
  (`fields` + `default` array).
- Every image is an image prop with real alt text. Every optional region (eyebrow, buttons, a card,
  a logo, a background) has a boolean prop and `if: "showX"` on the element — never on the section
  root or on a list's item template.
- Every component has a `theme` group from `themeVariant()` in `components/_lib/themes.js`, plus the
  layout groups the design needs (`align`, `size`, `columns`, …). Write base colors with `themeBase()`
  so the default option and the variant stay the same colors. A photo hero can use layout groups
  (`align`, `overlay`, `height`) instead of a color theme.
- `tree`: root is always `{ role: "section", style, children }`.

## Structure: always use roles for layout wrappers
```js
{ role: "gutter", children: [{ role: "container", size: "large", children: [
  { role: "section-padding", size: "large", children: [ { tag: "div", key: "component", ... } ] }
] }] }
```
Roles become framework classes (Client-First `padding-global` etc.). Never style a role node other
than the section. Full-bleed parts (marquees) can sit outside `gutter` but inside `section-padding`.

## Elements
- Tags: `div`, `h1`–`h4`, `p`, `a`, `img`. One `h1` only in heroes; sections use `h2`, items `h3`.
- Every element has a unique kebab-case `key`. The class becomes `prefix_key` (Client-First).
- Content comes from `bind`: a prop name, `item.<field>` inside a list, or `@index` (01, 02…).
  `text` is only for non-editable glyphs like "✳". `a` needs `href` (prop, `item.field`, or literal).
- `repeat: "<prop>"` on a container: for `buttons` it has no children; for `list` exactly one child
  (the item template). No nested lists.
- `decorative: true` for purely visual elements (adds aria-hidden).

## Styles (validator enforces these)
- Mobile-first `{ base, md, lg }` (md ≥768, lg ≥992).
- Longhand only: `padding-top`, `border-top-left-radius`, `grid-column-start`… never `padding`,
  `border`, `gap`, `flex`, `inset`, `background`.
- Any property set at `md`/`lg` must also exist in `base` (Webflow is desktop-first).
- Colors, fonts and radii use tokens: `"token(ink)"`. Available tokens: `src/core/tokens.js`.
  Add a token there rather than hard-coding a brand color.
- Prefer flex; use grid only for real 2D layouts. Keep CSS Webflow-compatible (no ::before, no
  keyframes, no selectors).

## Motion and behaviour
- `motion.enter` (staggered header entrance), `motion.reveal` (list items), `motion.scroll` (parallax).
- Runtime behaviours via `attrs`: `data-blok-marquee` (+ `data-blok-speed`, `data-blok-direction="reverse"`),
  `data-blok-count`, `data-blok-split="words"|"rise"`, `data-blok-reveal="clip"`, `data-blok-trail`,
  `data-blok-stack`, `data-blok-hscroll`, `data-blok-accordion`, `data-blok-billing`, `data-blok-compare`,
  `data-blok-progress`, `data-blok-fit`, `data-blok-tabs`, `data-blok-carousel`, `data-blok-filter`,
  `data-blok-spotlight`, `data-blok-expand`, `data-blok-lightbox`, `data-blok-swap`, `data-blok-meters`,
  `data-blok-cycle`, `data-blok-scrub`, `data-blok-hover-reveal` > `data-blok-hover-row` > `data-blok-hover-media`.
  An attr value starting with `=` binds a prop (`=heading`, `=item.field`). New behaviours go in
  `src/runtime/motion-core.js` and must respect reduced motion.

## Check your work
`npm run dev` (gallery with live reload) and `npm run check` (tests + TypeScript). Look at the
component at 390px and 1440px.
